      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "Min-jun",
          "text": "XYZ dipped today. Perfect time to buy more"
        },
        {
          "from": "Min-jun",
          "text": "I'm doing 영끌 this time. Loan + savings, all in"
        },
        {
          "from": "Ji-woo",
          "me": true,
          "text": "All in? With a loan?"
        },
        {
          "from": "Min-jun",
          "text": "The bank charges 6% a year. XYZ makes that in a week"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "t0",
      "title": "After work",
      "body": "Gangnam, after work. Min-jun has a new plan. This time he wants to borrow and put in everything. Ji-woo has ₩10 million of her own."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "t1",
      "kicker": "Word from Seoul",
      "title": "영끌 · yeongkkeul",
      "body": "Koreans have a word for this: yeongkkeul. Pulling in everything, even your soul. Korean media used it in 2020–21, when many young people borrowed all they could to buy homes and shares. Her own ₩10 million, plus ₩10 million borrowed. Investing with borrowed money is called [[leverage]]."
    },
    {
      "type": "slider-chart",
      "id": "s1",
      "concepts": [
        "leverage"
      ],
      "prompt": "Ji-woo invests her ₩10M and borrows more on top. Move the price: what happens to her own money? Find a fall that wipes out everything she had.",
      "model": "leverage",
      "params": {
        "move": {
          "label": "Price change",
          "min": -60,
          "max": 60,
          "step": 5,
          "value": 20,
          "unit": "%"
        },
        "borrowed": {
          "label": "Borrowed, as % of her own money",
          "min": 0,
          "max": 200,
          "step": 50,
          "value": 100,
          "unit": "%"
        }
      },
      "output": {
        "label": "Result on her own ₩10M",
        "unit": "%"
      },
      "goal": {
        "condition": {
          "of": "output",
          "max": -100
        },
        "hint": "With as much borrowed as she owns, the price only has to halve."
      },
      "note": "Simplified: no interest or fees."
    },
